// EXPECT: [E082]
// The measure decreases but is NOT well-founded: `n` is signed and the base case
// `n == 0` does not catch negatives, so `bad(-1)` recurses forever. The `>= 0`
// requirement rejects it (guard `n < 1`, or use an unsigned measure).
// (The step is explicitly WRAPPING so that no arithmetic refusal fires first and hides this
// test's subject, which is WELL-FOUNDEDNESS. `n - 1` on an unrefined i32 is a real underflow
// at INT_MIN, and once the sovereign engine owned the recursion verdict that E086 arrived
// before the termination one. Wrapping escapes the overflow obligation and never totality:
// a counter that wraps is not a well-founded witness either, so the program is still refused
// — for the reason the test is named after.)
func bad(n i32) i32 decreasing n {
    if n == 0 { return 0 }
    return bad(n -% 1)
}
func main() i32 { return bad(3) }
